**Theorem 14.3 (endpoint-sign sufficient orientation test).** Under
Assumption 10.1, the sufficient conditions

$$
R_-\succeq0,\qquad a_o^2+b_o^2>0,\qquad
a_o(a_o+\chi b_o)\ge0
\tag{FC.50}
$$

imply all conclusions of Theorem 14.1. They allow an endpoint zero
at $z=0$ or $z=\chi$.

Proof. The nonzero affine function $a_o+b_oz$ has weakly same-sign
values at $0,\chi$, and hence has a fixed strict sign on
$(0,\chi)$. Both endpoint values cannot be zero unless
$a_o=b_o=0$. By Theorem 13.1 the negative slope interval is
contained in this open interval. Thus the proof of Theorem 13.2
applies directly and Theorem 14.1 follows. This is a sufficient
orientation test on the larger interval $(0,\chi)$; no necessity
assertion is made. $\square$

